urleech
----------

Passive URL discovery from historical web archives and external intelligence
sources.

Queries external datasets for URLs previously observed on a target domain and
its subdomains.  It does not crawl target websites, make requests to target
hosts, execute JavaScript, or verify whether discovered URLs are currently
accessible.


Providers

    Providers run automatically.

        - Wayback Machine (CDX)
        - Common Crawl
        - AlienVault OTX
        - urlscan.io
        - VirusTotal


API keys

    Configured through environment variables.

        OTX_API_KEY
            Optional.  Used when querying AlienVault OTX.

        URLSCAN_API_KEY
            Optional.  Used for urlscan.io search and result access.
            Search quotas and historical visibility depend on the account.

        VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY
            Required for VirusTotal.  The domain URLs relationship is restricted
            to Premium API users.  Without an API key the provider is skipped.
            A non-Premium key may receive an authorization error.

    The following providers do not require API keys:

        - Common Crawl
        - Wayback Machine


Usage

    urleech [options] [domain ...]


Options

    -file <path>
        Read target domains from a file, one domain per line.

    -log <path>
        Write provider logs to the specified file.
        Defaults to ./urleech.log.

    -silent
        Disable application logging.


    If neither positional domains nor -file is supplied, urleech reads
    domains from stdin.


Examples

    urleech example.com

    urleech example.com example.org

    urleech -file domains.txt

    printf '%s\n' example.com example.org | urleech

    urleech -silent example.com

    urleech example.com > urls.jsonl


Input

    Domains can be supplied in three ways:

        - As positional command-line arguments.
        - Through a file specified with -file.
        - Through stdin.

    Input files and stdin accept one domain per line.  Empty lines are ignored.

    The -file option cannot be combined with positional domains.

    Domains are normalized before processing.  Leading and trailing whitespace
    and a trailing dot are removed, and domain names are converted to lowercase.
    Invalid domain names cause the application to exit with an error.


Output

    Results are written as JSON Lines (JSONL) to stdout. Each line represents
    one unique URL for a target domain.

    Each record contains:

        - schema_version
        - domain
        - url
        - evidence

    Logs are written to file, separately from the JSONL results, so that stdout
    can be redirected or piped into other tools.
